1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:14,001
It's at the core of our planet's $7 trillion construction industry,

2
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:16,860
sheltering more than half of the world's population.

3
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:18,960
Basically, it's the primary building material.

4
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:21,480
It teems with billions of living organisms

5
00:00:21,481 --> 00:00:24,500
and can move on its own with deadly results.

6
00:00:24,501 --> 00:00:29,240
The Dust Bowl in the 1930s was the greatest environmental disaster in American history.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,600
So whether you're playing in the mud

8
00:00:31,601 --> 00:00:35,760
or racing on it,

9
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:38,960
you'll never get your hands dirty the same way again

10
00:00:38,961 --> 00:00:41,420
on Modern Marvel's Down and Dirty.

11
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:56,160
You know the phrase, dull as dirt?

12
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:57,720
Well, it's not.

13
00:00:57,721 --> 00:01:00,620
We think of it just as a common thing.

14
00:01:00,621 --> 00:01:03,720
It's, I don't want my kids tracking it into the house.

15
00:01:03,721 --> 00:01:07,660
But if we didn't have dirt, we wouldn't have life on Earth.

16
00:01:07,661 --> 00:01:10,000
Just a little of it can support a universe.

17
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:14,200
A clump of soil this size has as many organisms in it

18
00:01:14,201 --> 00:01:16,180
as all the people on the Earth.

19
00:01:16,181 --> 00:01:19,780
But a lot of people on Earth live in dirt, too.

20
00:01:19,781 --> 00:01:23,320
It's used today to house about half the people

21
00:01:23,321 --> 00:01:24,480
that live on the planet Earth.

22
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:30,720
It's also purifier.

23
00:01:30,721 --> 00:01:33,720
Water drains through clean, good soil

24
00:01:33,721 --> 00:01:35,720
and comes out pure, clean.

25
00:01:35,721 --> 00:01:37,720
We can drink it.

26
00:01:37,721 --> 00:01:40,720
We spend a lot of time digging in dirt

27
00:01:40,721 --> 00:01:43,720
because it holds a wealth of valuable materials,

28
00:01:43,721 --> 00:01:47,720
and in it hide the secrets of ancient civilizations.

29
00:01:47,721 --> 00:01:51,720
Most of us are surrounded by dirt all of our lives

30
00:01:51,721 --> 00:01:54,720
and add to it when we die.

31
00:01:54,721 --> 00:02:01,280
To keep us on the move, we move an awful lot of dirt while building millions of miles of

32
00:02:01,281 --> 00:02:04,120
roads, highways, and freeways.

33
00:02:04,121 --> 00:02:10,680
Usually we top off transportation infrastructure with a layer of concrete or asphalt.

34
00:02:10,681 --> 00:02:19,800
But sometimes when we're in a sporting mood, it's all about driving on dirt.

35
00:02:19,801 --> 00:02:24,980
At 16 locations across North America each year, a lot of earth-moving expertise goes

36
00:02:24,981 --> 00:02:30,860
into the quick, yet precise transformation of pristine baseball or football stadiums

37
00:02:30,861 --> 00:02:36,700
into dirt obstacle courses for the motorsport event known as Supercross.

38
00:02:36,701 --> 00:02:41,400
It started in 1972 and is considered one of the first extreme motorsports.

39
00:02:41,401 --> 00:02:48,200
Supercross is basically taking an outdoor motocross track and condensing it into a football

40
00:02:48,201 --> 00:02:49,500
or baseball field.

41
00:02:49,501 --> 00:02:52,080
And when you condense it, obviously,

42
00:02:52,081 --> 00:02:54,420
you're going to have super obstacles.

43
00:02:54,421 --> 00:02:57,420
So that's why we call it Supercross.

44
00:02:57,421 --> 00:03:01,480
These guys come out here and they take a 400-pound motorcycle

45
00:03:01,481 --> 00:03:03,440
and make it look easy.

46
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:08,520
How do you build a Supercross course?

47
00:03:08,521 --> 00:03:13,480
We went to Sam Boyd Stadium in Las Vegas to find out.

48
00:03:13,481 --> 00:03:16,460
The first task is to protect the venue's nearly $2 million

49
00:03:16,461 --> 00:03:18,000
worth of synthetic turf.

50
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:23,600
That process includes laying down about 52 rolls of plastic down on the floor

51
00:03:23,601 --> 00:03:27,080
and importing 13 tractor-trailer loads,

52
00:03:27,380 --> 00:03:30,520
the equivalent of 6,500 sheets of plywood on this field.

53
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:33,140
Once we get the plywood down and in place,

54
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:35,820
we're going to import a road-based type material.

55
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:40,280
The road base serves as a work service,

56
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:42,440
able to support the heavy equipment.

57
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:46,380
Once it's in place, it's time to haul in the dirt for the track.

58
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:53,460
Just over those trees on the corner parking lot of the stadium is where we keep our dirt pile here in Las Vegas.

59
00:03:53,461 --> 00:03:54,864
Sets there all year long.

60
00:03:54,865 --> 00:03:58,460
We come in and march for Monster Jam World Finals, use the dirt.

61
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:06,460
Two months later, we're back in here with Supercross and back processing the dirt.

62
00:04:06,461 --> 00:04:12,460
Right now it's a 70-30 mixture. 70% clay, 30% sand.

63
00:04:12,461 --> 00:04:16,460
We add that sand in there to keep that moisture deep inside the clay,

64
00:04:16,461 --> 00:04:19,460
to keep that moisture during the track so the track doesn't dry out,

65
00:04:19,461 --> 00:04:22,460
it doesn't get hard packed, it doesn't get dusty for the fans.

66
00:04:25,460 --> 00:04:30,460
On a typical Supercross track, we use about 5,500 yards of dirt,

67
00:04:30,461 --> 00:04:33,460
which equates to about 500 tandem wheel dump truck loads.

68
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:38,460
This is freshly brought in from the stockpile.

69
00:04:38,461 --> 00:04:41,860
from the stockpile.

70
00:04:41,861 --> 00:04:44,420
As you can tell, it's very loose.

71
00:04:44,421 --> 00:04:47,220
It also has a lot of the clumping factors

72
00:04:47,221 --> 00:04:50,140
necessary to build the track and shape the jumps.

73
00:04:53,640 --> 00:04:56,180
Out here in Las Vegas in the middle of the desert,

74
00:04:56,181 --> 00:04:58,500
the water trucks will come in every few minutes

75
00:04:58,501 --> 00:04:59,940
and add moisture to the dirt.

76
00:04:59,941 --> 00:05:03,460
We have to do that in order for it to stay on the floor.

77
00:05:03,461 --> 00:05:05,560
If it gets too dusty, we get a big wind,

78
00:05:05,561 --> 00:05:09,800
this whole track could end up in the parking lot.

79
00:05:09,801 --> 00:05:12,360
Each Supercross course is designed and built

80
00:05:12,361 --> 00:05:15,860
by a company called, appropriately enough, Dirtworks.

81
00:05:15,861 --> 00:05:18,380
We sit down with Dirtworks and we develop a plan.

82
00:05:18,381 --> 00:05:20,620
It's never the same track every time we come to the venue.

83
00:05:20,621 --> 00:05:22,720
It's never the same track any other city,

84
00:05:22,721 --> 00:05:24,240
and it's never the same track every year.

85
00:05:24,241 --> 00:05:26,320
So it's very difficult and challenging

86
00:05:26,321 --> 00:05:28,780
to develop new tracks, new diagrams that are challenging,

87
00:05:28,781 --> 00:05:31,180
but yet safe to the riders.

88
00:05:31,181 --> 00:05:34,240
Front end loaders carry the dirt to areas where stakes

89
00:05:34,241 --> 00:05:36,580
mark the jumps that are to be built.

90
00:05:36,581 --> 00:05:39,980
Then the bulldozer operator is the one that actually shapes the jump,

91
00:05:39,981 --> 00:05:42,680
shapes it into its primary form.

92
00:05:42,681 --> 00:05:45,520
The dozer just running over it, running over the jump,

93
00:05:45,521 --> 00:05:47,520
basically compacts this dirt,

94
00:05:47,521 --> 00:05:52,260
and then they'll shape it with the blade of the dozer after that.

95
00:05:52,261 --> 00:05:54,560
And the smaller skid steers, the finishers,

96
00:05:54,561 --> 00:05:57,240
they'll come in and actually smooth it all out.

97
00:05:57,241 --> 00:06:00,100
What we call icing, they'll smooth the sides of the jumps down

98
00:06:00,101 --> 00:06:02,160
so it doesn't just look like a dirt pile there.

99
00:06:02,161 --> 00:06:06,760
If you notice out here, all the sides of the jumps are nice and iced like a cake.

100
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:11,840
What we're doing right now, as you can see behind me, is we're building the whoop section.

101
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:18,540
Whoop section is a series of small bumps about 36 inches tall and about 10 foot apart.

102
00:06:18,660 --> 00:06:20,240
There'll be 50 of these things built.

103
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:24,460
These whoop sections will challenge the riders unlike no other obstacle on the track.

104
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:30,780
The idea behind it is to carry your momentum, shift up, and stay on top and just skim across each face of them.

105
00:06:30,781 --> 00:06:33,620
If you drop your front end in, it might be over for you.

106
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:38,720
I'm standing by one of the larger obstacles on the Supercross track,

107
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:39,700
and that's the finish line.

108
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:42,320
As the Supercross competitors come flying through the section

109
00:06:42,321 --> 00:06:43,600
and clip up into fourth gear,

110
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:47,620
they'll be flying more than 75 feet to the landing,

111
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:50,000
and the takeoff is more than 10 feet tall.

112
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:53,420
It's very critical to get the degree and pitch just right.

113
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:58,120
He will do it! He will win his second AMA Supercross

114
00:06:58,121 --> 00:07:04,860
It's an FIF World Championship in one of the wildest championship seasons in Supercross history.

115
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:07,900
People say dirt's cheap. Dirt's not cheap.

116
00:07:08,060 --> 00:07:10,700
This is a pretty expensive operation you've got going on behind me,

117
00:07:10,800 --> 00:07:14,520
but people pack the stands and it makes it all worth it in the end.

118
00:07:15,240 --> 00:07:19,000
Many other sports, like horse racing, rely on well-chosen dirt, too.

119
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:25,440
Baseball fields use mixtures of sand, silt, and clay to create the best service.

120
00:07:25,441 --> 00:07:29,440
The pitcher's mound usually has a higher clay content

121
00:07:29,441 --> 00:07:31,180
so that it will hold its shape.

122
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:33,620
But there's another kind of dirt

123
00:07:33,621 --> 00:07:35,560
that's absolutely essential to pro baseball.

124
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:38,320
Every single baseball put into play

125
00:07:38,321 --> 00:07:40,200
at all major and minor league games

126
00:07:40,201 --> 00:07:44,000
must be first rubbed with Lena Blackburn rubbing mud.

127
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:47,460
We get issued our mud at the league meetings every year.

128
00:07:47,980 --> 00:07:51,020
I just know that it comes from a secret spot

129
00:07:51,021 --> 00:07:52,100
in a river in New Jersey.

130
00:07:52,101 --> 00:07:59,500
It's this man, Jim Bintliff of Southern New Jersey, who provides it.

131
00:07:59,501 --> 00:08:05,500
We're off to the secret location to get this year's rubbing mud.

132
00:08:05,501 --> 00:08:07,807
Supplying it isn't a full-time job.

133
00:08:07,808 --> 00:08:11,300
Jim works the night shift operating a printing press,

134
00:08:11,301 --> 00:08:15,620
leaving his days free to collect the mud pro baseball has come to rely on.

135
00:08:15,621 --> 00:08:24,500
In the late 1930s, the umpires were looking for a way to take the gloss off of new baseball,

136
00:08:24,501 --> 00:08:28,140
make it easier for the pitcher to grip, give them more control.

137
00:08:28,141 --> 00:08:33,620
Lena Blackburn, being a major league player and manager, knew of this mud, gave it to

138
00:08:33,621 --> 00:08:39,460
the umpires and found that it was perfect for what they were looking for.

139
00:08:39,461 --> 00:08:42,500
Lena was a good friend of my grandfather.

140
00:08:42,501 --> 00:08:46,500
When he passed away, he left the mud to my grandfather,

141
00:08:46,501 --> 00:08:50,320
who left it to my father, who left it to me.

142
00:08:50,321 --> 00:08:51,800
There's only a handful of people that

143
00:08:51,801 --> 00:08:53,960
know the location of the mud.

144
00:08:53,961 --> 00:08:58,460
And that's why you're going to turn the camera off right now.

145
00:08:58,461 --> 00:09:00,900
The tools of the trade are pretty basic.

146
00:09:00,901 --> 00:09:03,620
Five gallon plastic buckets, a shovel,

147
00:09:03,621 --> 00:09:07,540
and some soon to be worthless shoes.

148
00:09:07,541 --> 00:09:12,460
I've been collecting mud for 30 years plus.

149
00:09:12,461 --> 00:09:15,340
I collect three to six buckets a trip,

150
00:09:15,341 --> 00:09:19,920
and I provide about 900 pounds of mud every year.

151
00:09:19,921 --> 00:09:20,760
All right.

152
00:09:20,761 --> 00:09:24,640
The tide's got to be low, and tide's perfect.

153
00:09:24,641 --> 00:09:26,900
Now it's time to get dirty.

154
00:09:26,901 --> 00:09:29,560
Since this part of the river is near the ocean,

155
00:09:29,561 --> 00:09:31,300
it's affected by tides.

156
00:09:31,301 --> 00:09:34,860
When the tide goes out, the mud hole is revealed.

157
00:09:34,861 --> 00:09:37,440
This stuff is slippery, so Jim treads carefully down

158
00:09:37,441 --> 00:09:39,780
to where he harvests the mud.

159
00:09:39,781 --> 00:09:42,160
The thing that makes this mud so special

160
00:09:42,161 --> 00:09:51,160
is the mineral content is different than most other areas.

161
00:09:51,161 --> 00:09:58,240
Have a high content of feldspar, which is part of the Quartz family.

162
00:09:58,241 --> 00:10:00,720
It's the feldspar that gives the mud a gritty quality

163
00:10:00,721 --> 00:10:03,580
that scuffs the leather on the baseballs.

164
00:10:03,581 --> 00:10:05,740
Before there was an official rubbing mud,

165
00:10:05,741 --> 00:10:08,980
baseball tried dirt from the infield, shoe polish,

166
00:10:08,981 --> 00:10:10,540
and even tobacco juice.

167
00:10:10,541 --> 00:10:13,380
But they either damage the ball's leather or its laces.

168
00:10:13,381 --> 00:10:16,040
From what my parents have told me,

169
00:10:16,041 --> 00:10:20,980
Lena Blackburn discovered this because he used to fish

170
00:10:20,981 --> 00:10:23,420
and swim in this area.

171
00:10:23,421 --> 00:10:25,660
The reason I use the top layer of mud

172
00:10:25,661 --> 00:10:29,300
is it's a much cleaner mud.

173
00:10:29,301 --> 00:10:35,660
If I go too deep, the mud tends to get dark,

174
00:10:35,661 --> 00:10:38,340
and it gets a nasty smell.

175
00:10:38,341 --> 00:10:41,720
After filling his buckets, it's time to head home.

176
00:10:41,721 --> 00:10:47,080
As that tide comes up, the water will spill over into this area.

177
00:10:47,081 --> 00:10:51,780
It'll be over my head, and it'll cover my mud pit again,

178
00:10:51,781 --> 00:10:54,320
keeping the secret for me.

179
00:10:54,321 --> 00:10:56,860
Jim processes the mud in his backyard.

180
00:10:56,861 --> 00:10:58,680
The first step is to strain it to eliminate

181
00:10:58,681 --> 00:10:59,700
rocks and other debris.

182
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:05,700
But what happens next is another well-kept secret.

183
00:11:08,340 --> 00:11:13,340
Once he has a finished batch, he hand packs it and prepares it for shipment in his den.

184
00:11:13,341 --> 00:11:20,340
Right now I'm shipping approximately 400 containers a year.

185
00:11:20,341 --> 00:11:24,340
It's a dirty job, but somebody's got to do it.

186
00:11:24,341 --> 00:11:28,340
It was just a matter of time before someone in the show was going to say that.

187
00:11:28,341 --> 00:11:32,340
While the baseball rubbing mud is sought after for its sandpaper-like qualities,

188
00:11:32,341 --> 00:11:39,340
there's another so-called sport where abrasive dirt can be a real problem.

189
00:11:43,340 --> 00:11:47,340
In many sports, getting down in the dirt is all just part of the game.

190
00:11:47,341 --> 00:11:49,340
But this takes it to the extreme.

191
00:11:49,341 --> 00:11:56,340
For three decades, a mud wrestling troupe known as the Chicago Knockers performed all over the U.S.

192
00:11:56,341 --> 00:12:01,340
But when they signed on for the state fair in Hawaii, dirt was almost a deal breaker.

193
00:12:01,341 --> 00:12:05,180
When we're on the mainland, we use Illinois topsoil.

194
00:12:05,181 --> 00:12:08,380
It's sifted forest, comes right from the Mississippi River basin,

195
00:12:08,381 --> 00:12:10,640
and we love to use that Illinois topsoil.

196
00:12:10,641 --> 00:12:12,860
But because we're here in Hawaii,

197
00:12:12,861 --> 00:12:15,720
the Agriculture Department won't let us bring in the dirt.

198
00:12:15,721 --> 00:12:19,720
The Department of Agriculture has set in place pretty strict rules

199
00:12:19,721 --> 00:12:23,120
in bringing soils into the islands.

200
00:12:23,121 --> 00:12:27,060
This makes sense because bacteria and fungi let out loose

201
00:12:27,061 --> 00:12:30,640
in this environment could wreak havoc on the natural ecosystems.

202
00:12:30,641 --> 00:12:33,580
So we had to have special topsoil prepared for us,

203
00:12:33,581 --> 00:12:36,720
sifted 32 times to get all the rock sediment

204
00:12:36,721 --> 00:12:39,020
and volcanic ash out of it.

205
00:12:39,021 --> 00:12:43,280
Just take some sift of dirt here, add a little water,

206
00:12:43,281 --> 00:12:45,120
and we're ready to go for mud wrestling.

207
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:49,900
This troop takes its wrestling seriously,

208
00:12:49,901 --> 00:12:51,420
and as far as the dirt is concerned,

209
00:12:51,421 --> 00:12:52,960
it's a full contact sport.

210
00:12:54,500 --> 00:12:55,840
They would prefer to be tussling

211
00:12:55,841 --> 00:12:58,400
in their silky smooth Mississippi mud,

212
00:12:58,401 --> 00:13:01,100
But at least this stuff isn't scratching.

213
00:13:01,101 --> 00:13:03,200
First time we came to Hawaii 25 years ago,

214
00:13:03,201 --> 00:13:04,700
we used the local topsoil here.

215
00:13:04,701 --> 00:13:06,140
It was full of volcanic ash.

216
00:13:06,141 --> 00:13:08,160
It was like wrestling on sandpaper.

217
00:13:08,161 --> 00:13:10,140
Boy, did that stuff eat you up.

218
00:13:10,141 --> 00:13:13,180
The irises in my eyes were burned, my elbows, my knees.

219
00:13:13,181 --> 00:13:14,060
But that's all right.

220
00:13:14,061 --> 00:13:15,380
We're back.

221
00:13:15,381 --> 00:13:17,400
We had the same problem in England.

222
00:13:17,401 --> 00:13:20,300
When we wrestled there, their topsoil was so rocky,

223
00:13:20,301 --> 00:13:21,140
we couldn't use it.

224
00:13:21,141 --> 00:13:22,800
We had to end up using chocolate cake mix.

225
00:13:25,900 --> 00:13:28,300
While the audiences may not see the appeal of actually

226
00:13:28,301 --> 00:13:30,440
getting thrown down in the dirt.

227
00:13:30,441 --> 00:13:32,380
Not so for these wrestlers.

228
00:13:32,381 --> 00:13:35,140
I grew up playing and working on the farm,

229
00:13:35,141 --> 00:13:36,200
playing in the dirt.

230
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:41,540
Unlike city girls,

231
00:13:41,541 --> 00:13:43,580
I'm not afraid to get my fingernails dirty.

232
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:46,220
It's no big deal for me to get dirty.

233
00:13:46,221 --> 00:13:48,860
I was raised in Clinton, Mississippi on a catfish farm.

234
00:13:48,861 --> 00:13:51,300
I grew up living in the mud practically.

235
00:13:51,301 --> 00:13:52,400
Funnest times of my life.

236
00:13:52,401 --> 00:13:53,800
So, you know, wrestling in the mud,

237
00:13:53,801 --> 00:13:55,220
it just brings me back to my childhood

238
00:13:55,221 --> 00:13:57,940
and who doesn't miss their childhood, right?

239
00:13:57,941 --> 00:14:01,120
Getting down and dirty is part of the fun of mud wrestling.

240
00:14:01,121 --> 00:14:02,380
But how about living in it?

241
00:14:06,180 --> 00:14:08,560
Many animals engineer homes out of dirt,

242
00:14:08,561 --> 00:14:10,880
from towering African termite mounds

243
00:14:10,881 --> 00:14:13,520
to the tunnels dug by prairie dogs.

244
00:14:13,521 --> 00:14:14,320
And we do too.

245
00:14:17,620 --> 00:14:22,000
One of the oldest building materials still in use is adobe.

246
00:14:22,001 --> 00:14:23,700
Adobe originated in the Middle East,

247
00:14:23,701 --> 00:14:26,940
and the word atobe means mud brick.

248
00:14:26,941 --> 00:14:29,820
And it's been used in all parts of the world,

249
00:14:29,821 --> 00:14:32,760
basically as the primary building material.

250
00:14:32,761 --> 00:14:35,560
In some places, adobe bricks are still made by hand.

251
00:14:38,700 --> 00:14:41,880
But in Tucson, Arizona, clay mine adobe mass

252
00:14:41,881 --> 00:14:44,100
produces its bricks with the help of machines.

253
00:14:47,500 --> 00:14:50,720
Workers mix the batches of mud in what's called a pug mill.

254
00:14:50,721 --> 00:15:00,000
Modern adobe bricks usually contain some cement for added strength, but this batch is for

255
00:15:00,001 --> 00:15:06,660
a customer who wants bricks made with just the traditional ingredients, sand, clay, water,

256
00:15:06,661 --> 00:15:08,820
and straw.

257
00:15:08,821 --> 00:15:12,560
Straw is typically used in adobe to act as a binder.

258
00:15:12,561 --> 00:15:20,280
The pug mill operator uses a conveyor to add in the dirt ingredients, clay and sand.

259
00:15:20,281 --> 00:15:22,320
The ratio has to be just right.

260
00:15:22,321 --> 00:15:25,280
Too much clay will make bricks prone to shrinkage and cracking.

261
00:15:25,281 --> 00:15:28,420
Too much sand makes them crumbly.

262
00:15:28,421 --> 00:15:31,140
The pug mill has a scale that lets the operator know

263
00:15:31,141 --> 00:15:33,180
how much of each he's adding.

264
00:15:33,181 --> 00:15:37,800
And then he'll wait and let the mix settle down in the pug mill

265
00:15:37,801 --> 00:15:39,780
and let the consistency even out.

266
00:15:39,781 --> 00:15:41,760
And then he knows it's time to bring in

267
00:15:41,761 --> 00:15:44,300
either a touch more water or a little bit more dirt

268
00:15:44,301 --> 00:15:46,400
to get the mix just right.

269
00:15:46,401 --> 00:15:48,600
In Adobe brick making jargon, the mix

270
00:15:48,601 --> 00:15:50,800
has to have the right slump.

271
00:15:50,801 --> 00:15:53,900
The term slump is just how thick your mud is.

272
00:15:53,901 --> 00:15:55,800
Is it like brownie mix or is it soupy?

273
00:15:58,680 --> 00:16:00,740
Once the mix is the right consistency,

274
00:16:00,741 --> 00:16:03,440
it's time to make bricks.

275
00:16:03,441 --> 00:16:06,160
The finished mix is now coming into the car

276
00:16:06,161 --> 00:16:08,860
that distributes the mud on the bridge.

277
00:16:08,861 --> 00:16:11,320
All of the forms are displayed in the bridge.

278
00:16:11,321 --> 00:16:14,700
And so there's a valve at the bottom of the car

279
00:16:14,701 --> 00:16:16,360
that opens up and lets the mud out.

280
00:16:16,361 --> 00:16:20,200
and the vibrator comes on, and the mud all goes down and fills the forms.

281
00:16:20,680 --> 00:16:25,880
And then there's a roller mechanism that pushes it into the forms

282
00:16:25,881 --> 00:16:29,580
so that when the operator looks and he sees, okay, they're all filled up,

283
00:16:29,960 --> 00:16:33,360
maybe one helper comes over with a shovel and makes sure they're all filled up.

284
00:16:36,180 --> 00:16:39,760
The car may make several trips back and forth to fill in all the forms.

285
00:16:39,940 --> 00:16:42,680
Then the operator lifts the forms from around the bricks

286
00:16:42,681 --> 00:16:45,820
and rolls the whole mechanism into position for the next batch.

287
00:16:46,360 --> 00:16:49,360
Each row we do about 105 bricks.

288
00:16:49,361 --> 00:16:52,360
On a good day we can do 5,000 or 6,000 of them.

289
00:16:52,361 --> 00:16:55,360
In a good year we'll probably do 250,000 of these.

290
00:16:55,361 --> 00:17:01,360
In cities like Tucson, building with adobe isn't just stylish,

291
00:17:01,361 --> 00:17:03,360
it's practical.

292
00:17:03,361 --> 00:17:06,360
Adobe works the best in the desert southwest

293
00:17:06,361 --> 00:17:09,360
because we're basically concerned with what's going on in the summer.

294
00:17:09,361 --> 00:17:11,360
It's hot, you want to stay cool.

295
00:17:11,361 --> 00:17:13,360
When the sun hits the outside of the adobe,

296
00:17:13,361 --> 00:17:17,600
the adobe, it travels into the house at about an inch per hour.

297
00:17:17,601 --> 00:17:20,140
So it really never gets to the inside of the house.

298
00:17:20,141 --> 00:17:22,800
At night, the sun goes down and the temperature

299
00:17:22,801 --> 00:17:25,000
starts going away.

300
00:17:25,001 --> 00:17:26,780
But adobe mud bricks aren't the only way

301
00:17:26,781 --> 00:17:30,080
to trick out your stylish new crib.

302
00:17:30,081 --> 00:17:30,781
Dirt.

303
00:17:30,782 --> 00:17:32,740
Some ride their motorcycles on it.

304
00:17:32,741 --> 00:17:35,520
Others like to wrestle in it.

305
00:17:35,521 --> 00:17:38,320
But millions around the globe build their homes with it.

306
00:17:38,321 --> 00:17:44,120
Adobe may be the world's most common building material,

307
00:17:44,121 --> 00:17:46,860
but there's another way to make sturdy walls out of dirt.

308
00:17:47,860 --> 00:17:51,340
Rammed earth is a building technique that's been around for thousands of years.

309
00:17:51,900 --> 00:17:54,380
Some examples are the Great Wall of China,

310
00:17:55,620 --> 00:17:56,700
Tibetan temples,

311
00:17:57,240 --> 00:18:01,940
the big infill walls for the castles in Europe that are then lined with stone.

312
00:18:04,960 --> 00:18:07,840
Quentin Branch, a builder of rammed earth structures,

313
00:18:07,841 --> 00:18:10,960
is currently working on his own home in Oracle, Arizona.

314
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:13,520
The Bobcat is our main tool.

315
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,200
We use the Bobcat to measure the dirt.

316
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,480
The Bobcat carries one ton of dirt in each scoop of the bucket.

317
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:22,920
I lay out 10 tons of dirt,

318
00:18:23,440 --> 00:18:27,060
and then I add Portland cement to stabilize the dirt.

319
00:18:27,560 --> 00:18:32,500
Dry mix it first, and now we mix in about 9% moisture.

320
00:18:34,240 --> 00:18:35,940
To make the walls of the structure,

321
00:18:35,941 --> 00:18:38,940
the bobcat dumps the earth into wood forms.

322
00:18:38,941 --> 00:18:40,940
Workers spread it out into an 8-inch layer.

323
00:18:44,940 --> 00:18:48,940
Then they use pneumatic tampers to compress the 8 inches down to 5 inches.

324
00:18:48,941 --> 00:18:51,940
They keep repeating the process until the form is full.

325
00:18:51,941 --> 00:18:56,940
After we fill these forms in with 5-inch compacted layers,

326
00:18:56,941 --> 00:19:01,940
we set another layer of forms on top,

327
00:19:01,941 --> 00:19:04,940
so then the wall will be at 8 feet.

328
00:19:05,940 --> 00:19:09,540
fill the forms up to eight feet, pull out from under,

329
00:19:09,541 --> 00:19:12,380
reset on top, and go up to 12 feet.

330
00:19:12,381 --> 00:19:15,940
We just keep leapfrogging the forms up the wall.

331
00:19:15,941 --> 00:19:19,020
The best soil for rammed earth is a soil

332
00:19:19,021 --> 00:19:22,460
that has sharp particles in it, and a good cross grade

333
00:19:22,461 --> 00:19:25,060
of different sizes of particles of dirt.

334
00:19:25,061 --> 00:19:27,860
The tamping creates such strong friction

335
00:19:27,861 --> 00:19:30,860
between the particles that it's like making

336
00:19:30,861 --> 00:19:33,860
a big sandstone block.

337
00:19:33,861 --> 00:19:38,540
The house should last for hundreds of years.

338
00:19:38,541 --> 00:19:40,240
The thick walls of rammed earth homes

339
00:19:40,241 --> 00:19:43,060
help keep the summer heat out like adobe.

340
00:19:43,061 --> 00:19:45,280
But there's yet another more unusual way

341
00:19:45,281 --> 00:19:48,680
to beat the desert's extreme temperatures.

342
00:19:48,681 --> 00:19:51,980
America's southwest is also home to strange structures

343
00:19:51,981 --> 00:19:53,180
called earthships.

344
00:19:56,780 --> 00:19:58,720
These environmentally friendly buildings

345
00:19:58,721 --> 00:20:01,560
feature walls made out of discarded steel-belted radial

346
00:20:01,561 --> 00:20:05,155
tires filled with what else? Compacted dirt.

347
00:20:05,156 --> 00:20:09,240
The tires themselves are about 30, 32 inches thick

348
00:20:09,880 --> 00:20:15,480
and each tire has about three and a half wheel barrels of dirt pounded inside solid and each

349
00:20:15,481 --> 00:20:22,280
tire weighs over 300 pounds when it's completed so it's self-standing there's no supports in there

350
00:20:22,281 --> 00:20:24,920
the house stands on its own from the friction.

351
00:20:24,921 --> 00:20:34,460
once the dirt filled tire walls are up the gaps are filled in with adobe and recycled aluminum

352
00:20:34,461 --> 00:20:38,360
cans and then the walls are finished off with a final coat of adobe

353
00:20:38,361 --> 00:20:48,020
and the walls will be perfectly smooth you'll see no tires you'll smell no tires you'll hear no

354
00:20:48,021 --> 00:20:53,580
tires building the thick walls next to a hillside effectively eliminates the need for heating and

355
00:20:53,581 --> 00:20:56,580
air conditioning, even in the temperature extremes of the desert.

356
00:20:56,581 --> 00:21:02,300
The earth keeps about a 72 degree temperature about 8 and a half feet down.

357
00:21:02,301 --> 00:21:07,060
These walls are 8 and a half feet hollow and they're berm completely behind with dirt.

358
00:21:07,061 --> 00:21:12,520
So the house theoretically, according to the architect, will not get above 80 degrees,

359
00:21:12,521 --> 00:21:18,740
even the 101 degree temperature outside.

360
00:21:18,741 --> 00:21:23,180
Of course we've used dirt to build much larger structures than earthships, rammed earth homes

361
00:21:23,181 --> 00:21:24,920
and adobe houses.

362
00:21:24,921 --> 00:21:27,140
And we've created all sorts of enormous equipment

363
00:21:27,141 --> 00:21:29,180
to move this stuff on a grand scale.

364
00:21:31,820 --> 00:21:34,000
Even a medium-sized dam like the Prado

365
00:21:34,001 --> 00:21:37,100
Dam in Southern California, built to stop floodwaters,

366
00:21:37,101 --> 00:21:39,680
depends on an impressive amount of dirt.

367
00:21:39,681 --> 00:21:41,720
Beneath the thin layer of rock that protects it

368
00:21:41,721 --> 00:21:46,100
from wind and water erosion are about 150 million cubic feet

369
00:21:46,101 --> 00:21:48,260
of clay and sand.

370
00:21:48,261 --> 00:21:50,720
To get a sense for how much material that is,

371
00:21:50,721 --> 00:21:52,580
if you consider a football field,

372
00:21:52,581 --> 00:21:57,720
That's approximately 3,000 feet high stacked up within the area of that football field.

373
00:21:58,520 --> 00:22:03,700
Now imagine building this sort of enormous structure with muscle instead of modern machinery.

374
00:22:05,480 --> 00:22:08,740
Surprisingly, it happened in what is now the central United States.

375
00:22:08,741 --> 00:22:17,460
From about 700 to 1400 AD, North America's largest ancient mound-building population lived in what is now called Cahokia,

376
00:22:19,020 --> 00:22:22,280
located near the Mississippi River in southwest Illinois.

377
00:22:22,580 --> 00:22:30,860
At its peak, the city of Cahokia covered nearly six square miles and had a population

378
00:22:30,861 --> 00:22:33,060
of about 20,000 people.

379
00:22:33,061 --> 00:22:38,780
The builders created over 120 mounds with more than 50 million cubic feet of earth, all

380
00:22:38,781 --> 00:22:42,220
dug with primitive tools and carried in baskets.

381
00:22:42,221 --> 00:22:47,560
At the center of this historical site is the largest mound, called Monk's Mound, made

382
00:22:47,561 --> 00:22:50,740
with over 22 million cubic feet of dirt.

383
00:22:50,741 --> 00:22:53,640
This is the largest prehistoric earthwork in the Americas.

384
00:22:53,641 --> 00:22:55,840
The base of the mound covers over 14 acres,

385
00:22:55,841 --> 00:22:58,740
which is even greater in size than the Great Pyramid of Egypt.

386
00:22:58,741 --> 00:23:00,140
It's not as tall, though.

387
00:23:00,141 --> 00:23:02,220
It's 100 feet high.

388
00:23:02,221 --> 00:23:04,120
It was entirely built by the Indians,

389
00:23:04,121 --> 00:23:06,120
carrying basket loads of dirt,

390
00:23:06,121 --> 00:23:09,100
perhaps 50 to 60 pounds at a time.

391
00:23:09,101 --> 00:23:12,760
It's estimated that it took about 15 million basket loads of dirt

392
00:23:12,761 --> 00:23:14,540
to build this mound.

393
00:23:14,541 --> 00:23:17,200
Over the centuries, the mound suffered from erosion,

394
00:23:17,201 --> 00:23:20,600
and some were reduced or removed by later inhabitants.

395
00:23:20,601 --> 00:23:23,700
so the original sizes are uncertain.

396
00:23:23,701 --> 00:23:26,840
But Cahokia holds an even greater mystery.

397
00:23:26,841 --> 00:23:30,780
What happened to this once thriving city?

398
00:23:30,781 --> 00:23:33,920
Starting around 1200 AD, we start to see a decline

399
00:23:33,921 --> 00:23:35,820
in population here at Cahokia.

400
00:23:35,821 --> 00:23:38,520
But by 1400, the site had been essentially abandoned,

401
00:23:38,521 --> 00:23:41,280
and we don't know where all the people went.

402
00:23:41,281 --> 00:23:43,580
Theories include warfare, climate change,

403
00:23:43,581 --> 00:23:46,120
and the depletion of the local natural resources,

404
00:23:46,121 --> 00:23:49,000
including fertile soil.

405
00:23:49,001 --> 00:23:51,500
And we could be facing the same issues today.

406
00:23:54,840 --> 00:23:57,380
When it comes to agriculture, dirt's name

407
00:23:57,381 --> 00:23:59,780
rises in stature to soil.

408
00:23:59,781 --> 00:24:02,220
Perhaps one of the best metaphors for soil

409
00:24:02,221 --> 00:24:04,280
is the Earth's skin.

410
00:24:04,281 --> 00:24:07,760
Our skin protects us, and soil protects the Earth

411
00:24:07,761 --> 00:24:10,220
that sustains life.

412
00:24:10,221 --> 00:24:12,520
And our Earth is pretty thin-skinned

413
00:24:12,521 --> 00:24:14,160
because the plant growth we depend on

414
00:24:14,161 --> 00:24:18,000
happens on the very surface, in the topsoil.

415
00:24:18,001 --> 00:24:23,400
It's full of organic material, including decomposed plants and animals and organisms.

416
00:24:23,401 --> 00:24:30,300
The bacteria and fungi are very important in soils because they decompose organic matter

417
00:24:30,301 --> 00:24:36,560
and convert it from useless nutrients to useful nutrients for plants.

418
00:24:37,320 --> 00:24:41,920
Scientists have identified over 20,000 varieties of soil in the United States.

419
00:24:42,440 --> 00:24:44,840
They include some of the richest topsoils in the world,

420
00:24:44,841 --> 00:24:47,400
from the nutrient-rich soils of the upper Midwest

421
00:24:47,401 --> 00:24:50,100
to the acidic volcanic soils in Hawaii

422
00:24:50,101 --> 00:24:52,320
that are perfect for growing pineapples.

423
00:24:54,480 --> 00:24:56,740
California's 400-mile-long Central Valley

424
00:24:56,741 --> 00:24:58,420
contains very productive soils

425
00:24:58,421 --> 00:25:00,940
that allow everything from grapes to cotton

426
00:25:00,941 --> 00:25:02,180
to flourish there.

427
00:25:04,240 --> 00:25:06,540
Our soils are alluvial soils,

428
00:25:06,780 --> 00:25:09,760
which means it was carried from rivers centuries ago

429
00:25:09,761 --> 00:25:11,560
from the Sierra Nevada Mountains,

430
00:25:11,561 --> 00:25:14,940
and that makes our soils very deep and very rich.

431
00:25:15,720 --> 00:25:19,460
Worldwide, rich, farmable soil is the exception and not the rule.

432
00:25:20,320 --> 00:25:21,940
Irrigation and fertilizers help,

433
00:25:22,320 --> 00:25:25,300
but overall we're losing it faster than nature can make it.

434
00:25:26,400 --> 00:25:31,020
Farmland is being lost at a rate of about 38,000 square miles per year

435
00:25:31,021 --> 00:25:35,540
due to erosion, urban encroachment, and other types of land conversion.

436
00:25:36,560 --> 00:25:39,420
Only about 10% of the earth's surface is farmable,

437
00:25:39,421 --> 00:25:41,620
but the population keeps increasing.

438
00:25:42,140 --> 00:25:44,060
That's why we have to protect what we have.

439
00:25:45,980 --> 00:25:49,020
About 90 years ago, farmers in America's Midwest

440
00:25:49,021 --> 00:25:52,660
found out the hard way how important it is to protect topsoil.

441
00:25:53,340 --> 00:25:57,900
The Dust Bowl in the 1930s was the greatest environmental disaster in American history.

442
00:25:58,480 --> 00:26:01,460
Most of the 1930s was a period of prolonged drought.

443
00:26:02,280 --> 00:26:05,280
Historically, this part of the U.S. had experienced droughts before,

444
00:26:05,900 --> 00:26:08,620
but the farmers that settled the area didn't know that.

445
00:26:08,621 --> 00:26:11,620
They became known as sodbusters.

446
00:26:11,621 --> 00:26:16,060
This is basically what the Blackland Prairie of Texas looked like before farmers came here

447
00:26:16,061 --> 00:26:21,020
and before they started plying up the subsoil and the topsoil.

448
00:26:21,021 --> 00:26:23,420
You had nothing but prairie grasses.

449
00:26:23,421 --> 00:26:26,760
This is what held everything together.

450
00:26:26,761 --> 00:26:32,600
They figured they would just plow it over and plant the way they had in the east.

451
00:26:32,601 --> 00:26:36,240
This worked as long as it rained, but when it stopped raining, there was nothing left

452
00:26:36,241 --> 00:26:38,240
to hold the soil in place.

453
00:26:38,241 --> 00:26:42,000
Nature took thousands of years to make the topsoil on the plains,

454
00:26:42,001 --> 00:26:44,380
but the winds just swept it away.

455
00:26:44,381 --> 00:26:47,120
The worst hit region was the southern plains,

456
00:26:47,121 --> 00:26:50,420
which extend north from north Texas, the Texas Panhandle,

457
00:26:50,421 --> 00:26:53,380
through Oklahoma and Kansas into southern Nebraska.

458
00:26:53,381 --> 00:26:58,060
And probably the reason for this was this is where the drought was the most severe.

459
00:26:58,061 --> 00:27:03,300
About 100 million acres of the southern plains turned into a wasteland.

460
00:27:03,301 --> 00:27:07,300
There'd be nothing to feed the animals, there'd be nothing to grow,

461
00:27:07,301 --> 00:27:10,280
There'd be no soil left to grow anything in.

462
00:27:10,680 --> 00:27:12,640
You'd be left with nothing but your house.

463
00:27:15,200 --> 00:27:18,920
The dust getting into your lungs would be the exact same thing

464
00:27:18,921 --> 00:27:20,840
as what the miners were going through out east.

465
00:27:21,040 --> 00:27:23,080
Instead of black lung, they would have dirt lung.

466
00:27:25,720 --> 00:27:28,600
Over 2 million people decided to give up and leave.

467
00:27:29,380 --> 00:27:32,880
Food prices rose nationwide, deepening the Great Depression.

468
00:27:33,620 --> 00:27:35,820
But from crisis came solutions.

469
00:27:35,821 --> 00:27:40,820
The Dust Bowl really helped to create the Soil Conservation Service.

470
00:27:40,821 --> 00:27:46,820
And one of the things the Soil Conservation Service has recommended is planting trees as wind breaks.

471
00:27:46,821 --> 00:27:49,820
That helped greatly to reduce wind erosion.

472
00:27:49,821 --> 00:27:54,820
I'm in an apple orchard now, and as you can see, we let the native grasses grow here.

473
00:27:54,821 --> 00:28:02,820
Years ago, farmers thought the weeds would compete against their crop and would use herbicides and spray the ground clean.

474
00:28:02,821 --> 00:28:05,160
Little did they know they were killing their soil

475
00:28:05,161 --> 00:28:07,280
because there was nothing to feed the soil microbes.

476
00:28:08,620 --> 00:28:12,060
While farmers have adopted many new methods to care for their topsoil,

477
00:28:12,520 --> 00:28:15,720
most homeowners seem more concerned about the appearance of their yards

478
00:28:15,721 --> 00:28:17,080
than the health of their soil.

479
00:28:17,620 --> 00:28:19,300
When you take away your grass clippings,

480
00:28:19,380 --> 00:28:20,940
when you take away the dead fruit on the ground,

481
00:28:21,100 --> 00:28:22,680
when you do the weeds and all that kind of stuff,

482
00:28:22,740 --> 00:28:24,880
you're taking away that natural cycle that nature has.

483
00:28:26,460 --> 00:28:29,380
Homeowners are able to replenish the lost organic material

484
00:28:29,381 --> 00:28:31,680
with soil amendments and potting soils.

485
00:28:31,681 --> 00:28:34,860
made by companies like Kellogg Garden Products.

486
00:28:35,920 --> 00:28:38,400
Trucks arrive at its Lockford, California facility,

487
00:28:38,920 --> 00:28:41,480
are lifted up, and sawdust and wood waste

488
00:28:41,481 --> 00:28:42,780
acquired from other businesses,

489
00:28:43,280 --> 00:28:45,980
including lumber mills and door and window manufacturers,

490
00:28:46,520 --> 00:28:47,220
pour out.

491
00:28:49,960 --> 00:28:52,200
This serves as the base organic matter

492
00:28:52,201 --> 00:28:53,620
for most of Kellogg's products.

493
00:28:56,060 --> 00:28:59,200
A front-end loader dumps the wood material into a hopper.

494
00:29:01,680 --> 00:29:06,620
that pours it onto a large shaker screen.

495
00:29:08,080 --> 00:29:09,920
The wood particles that are fine enough

496
00:29:09,921 --> 00:29:11,420
fall through the quarter-inch mesh.

497
00:29:11,980 --> 00:29:14,020
The big stuff will be ground up and used later.

498
00:29:17,100 --> 00:29:19,160
Now that the particle size is fine enough

499
00:29:19,161 --> 00:29:21,540
for our potting soil, we put iron into it.

500
00:29:21,760 --> 00:29:24,260
This is granulated iron now that will become available

501
00:29:24,261 --> 00:29:24,980
to the plants.

502
00:29:25,260 --> 00:29:27,640
Here in California, especially in most of the West Coast,

503
00:29:27,900 --> 00:29:29,760
the pH is too high, the iron is just not available

504
00:29:29,761 --> 00:29:30,461
for the plant.

505
00:29:31,680 --> 00:29:35,880
The conveyor carries the material to the back of the property

506
00:29:35,881 --> 00:29:39,480
and deposits it into huge piles.

507
00:29:39,481 --> 00:29:43,600
Other potting soil ingredients include nitrogen, rich bat guano,

508
00:29:43,601 --> 00:29:44,960
and earthworm castings.

509
00:29:44,961 --> 00:29:46,720
Worm castings are nothing more than worm poop,

510
00:29:46,721 --> 00:29:49,060
but they're really great because they add fertility

511
00:29:49,061 --> 00:29:51,240
and at the same time hormones that actually

512
00:29:51,241 --> 00:29:53,360
stimulate root growth.

513
00:29:53,361 --> 00:29:57,440
Then the mixture composts for three to four months.

514
00:29:57,441 --> 00:29:59,240
And if I dig into this, what you're going to see

515
00:29:59,241 --> 00:30:00,540
is all this steam coming out.

516
00:30:00,541 --> 00:30:01,540
See it coming out?

517
00:30:01,541 --> 00:30:03,540
That's because it's gotten so hot.

518
00:30:03,541 --> 00:30:07,000
The microbes in there start breaking down all those good materials we put together, all

519
00:30:07,001 --> 00:30:10,700
that good organic stuff, and as they break it down, they release heat as energy.

520
00:30:10,701 --> 00:30:15,720
And this temperature in here can get up to 140, 150 degrees.

521
00:30:15,721 --> 00:30:23,300
Once the pile is composted, it's loaded into the hoppers outside the packing plant.

522
00:30:23,301 --> 00:30:26,880
This is our finished potting, so we're adding some water to it, then going up here, we're

523
00:30:26,881 --> 00:30:27,880
adding perlite.

524
00:30:27,881 --> 00:30:30,880
Now perlite is a rock that's expanded during heat,

525
00:30:30,881 --> 00:30:32,080
and it's kind of like popcorn.

526
00:30:32,081 --> 00:30:33,080
It blows up.

527
00:30:33,081 --> 00:30:35,720
And what that does for us is it adds air spaces

528
00:30:35,721 --> 00:30:38,120
in the potting soil so it doesn't compact.

529
00:30:42,120 --> 00:30:44,440
Machines help workers bag the potting soils

530
00:30:44,441 --> 00:30:46,640
and other products inside the packing plant.

531
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:50,760
On this machine, we produce about 26 bags a minute.

532
00:30:50,761 --> 00:30:52,680
Overall production, with all different products,

533
00:30:52,681 --> 00:30:55,640
we produce about 25,000 to 30,000 units in a day.

534
00:30:55,641 --> 00:30:58,640
This is one of three Kellogg's facilities.

535
00:30:58,641 --> 00:31:01,640
The total yearly output is about 10 million bags.

536
00:31:01,641 --> 00:31:04,640
They're shipped all over the western United States

537
00:31:04,641 --> 00:31:06,640
and help keep gardens green and lush.

538
00:31:07,640 --> 00:31:11,640
Even dirt has the ability to become something beautiful.

539
00:31:11,641 --> 00:31:14,640
For thousands of years, clay has been used to make pottery

540
00:31:14,641 --> 00:31:16,640
and other ceramics.

541
00:31:16,641 --> 00:31:19,640
Silicate sand is melted to create glassworks.

542
00:31:20,640 --> 00:31:24,640
But Scott Wade of Wimberley, Texas, uses common everyday dirt

543
00:31:24,641 --> 00:31:31,640
everyday dirt to make his works of art on an unusual canvas, a dirty windshield.

544
00:31:31,641 --> 00:31:40,940
Of course this means one rainstorm and it's time to start a new one.

545
00:31:40,941 --> 00:31:45,800
Prep for this artist means driving back and forth on dirt roads near his home.

546
00:31:45,801 --> 00:31:51,380
This dirt that is on this road makes this great fine dust.

547
00:31:51,381 --> 00:31:54,380
It's limestone and a little bit of clay mixed in,

548
00:31:54,381 --> 00:31:58,180
helps it stick, and it's just very powdery.

549
00:31:58,181 --> 00:31:59,460
It's great to work with.

550
00:31:59,461 --> 00:32:03,320
It just adheres to the glass over the course of driving

551
00:32:03,321 --> 00:32:05,620
a week or so down the dirt road,

552
00:32:05,621 --> 00:32:09,140
and makes a terrific canvas for dirty car art.

553
00:32:12,300 --> 00:32:14,160
Scott makes it a point to buy cars

554
00:32:14,161 --> 00:32:16,140
with a type of upright rear windows

555
00:32:16,141 --> 00:32:17,540
that best catch the dust.

556
00:32:19,540 --> 00:32:20,980
Got my canvas all prepared.

557
00:32:20,981 --> 00:32:23,720
I've got my tools, a rubber paint shaper tool,

558
00:32:23,721 --> 00:32:27,320
sorted brushes, whatever might work

559
00:32:27,321 --> 00:32:28,260
for this particular piece.

560
00:32:28,261 --> 00:32:29,480
I've got my visual reference.

561
00:32:29,481 --> 00:32:32,560
I'm ready to start, so let's go.

562
00:32:32,561 --> 00:32:34,420
His drawings include original works

563
00:32:34,421 --> 00:32:35,860
and creations of everything,

564
00:32:37,060 --> 00:32:39,440
from dusty classic paintings by the masters

565
00:32:40,400 --> 00:32:42,160
to dirty dogs playing poker.

566
00:32:42,161 --> 00:32:44,500
I have a degree in commercial art

567
00:32:44,501 --> 00:32:47,640
and I work as a graphical user interface designer.

568
00:32:47,641 --> 00:32:49,740
This kind of was a hobby for me

569
00:32:49,741 --> 00:32:51,580
because I'd come home from work and, you know,

570
00:32:51,581 --> 00:32:52,840
it's kind of a high-stress job,

571
00:32:52,841 --> 00:32:54,940
working at a computer, sitting at a desk.

572
00:32:54,941 --> 00:32:57,740
So this gave me an opportunity to kind of get outside

573
00:32:57,741 --> 00:32:58,780
and do some stuff.

574
00:32:58,781 --> 00:33:00,520
I just always had dirty car windows.

575
00:33:01,580 --> 00:33:03,480
Scott has been doing his dirty car art

576
00:33:03,481 --> 00:33:05,520
for as long as he can remember.

577
00:33:05,521 --> 00:33:07,320
But it became more than just a hobby

578
00:33:07,321 --> 00:33:09,860
when someone posted some of his work on the internet.

579
00:33:11,120 --> 00:33:12,900
It went kind of viral.

580
00:33:12,901 --> 00:33:16,360
And it just surprised me to no end,

581
00:33:16,361 --> 00:33:17,960
because I thought to myself,

582
00:33:17,961 --> 00:33:20,260
Why would anybody want to look at dirty pictures on the internet?

583
00:33:20,980 --> 00:33:24,200
Ever since, Scott has worked on advertising campaigns

584
00:33:24,201 --> 00:33:26,160
and is in demand at art festivals.

585
00:33:29,900 --> 00:33:34,480
And for a show about dirt, I give you a handful of dirt.

586
00:33:37,520 --> 00:33:39,820
And what can you do with just a handful of dirt?

587
00:33:40,560 --> 00:33:42,140
How about rub it all over yourself?

588
00:33:42,720 --> 00:33:45,520
Lots of people do, and they're willing to pay for the privilege.

589
00:33:45,521 --> 00:33:54,120
Well, for a time immemorial, mud has been used for therapeutic value, cleansing, and ritual uses.

590
00:33:54,121 --> 00:34:00,320
The Glen Ivy Spa in Southern California offers a variety of treatments.

591
00:34:00,321 --> 00:34:04,620
But the big draw is Club Mud.

592
00:34:04,621 --> 00:34:09,020
When our guests come to use Club Mud, the first thing they do is enter the pool right here.

593
00:34:09,021 --> 00:34:13,020
It's easiest when your skin is a little bit wet and the mud's going to go on a lot smoother.

594
00:34:13,021 --> 00:34:17,580
They just grab a handful of mud from the center, rub a thin layer all over.

595
00:34:17,720 --> 00:34:19,560
You can put it from your head to your toes.

596
00:34:21,160 --> 00:34:27,880
The red clay comes from a local source and mixed with our water makes this very fine paste.

597
00:34:28,180 --> 00:34:38,431
The secret to this is it's the, don't tell anybody, but it's the same material that roof tiles and paving stones and bricks are made out of without any of the

598
00:34:38,432 --> 00:34:39,132
additives.

599
00:34:39,133 --> 00:34:42,460
It's just the fine red clay with a high iron content.

600
00:34:43,020 --> 00:34:47,060
To mix up a batch of clay, all it takes is water and bare feet.

601
00:34:48,080 --> 00:34:52,220
We bring in about 40 tons a year of the dry clay.

602
00:34:52,580 --> 00:34:56,600
This is going to be mixed up to a consistency of pizza dough.

603
00:34:58,420 --> 00:35:01,780
After Club Mud's patrons have smeared the clay all over themselves,

604
00:35:02,060 --> 00:35:04,580
it's time to take it easy and let it dry.

605
00:35:09,080 --> 00:35:12,320
Then the most important step, rubbing it off.

606
00:35:12,321 --> 00:35:17,000
The mud's an exfoliating treatment, so it's going to get all the impurities and dirt and toxins out of your pores,

607
00:35:17,001 --> 00:35:19,700
as well as take all the dead, dry skin off the surface.

608
00:35:19,701 --> 00:35:23,820
So when people are done showering off, their skin is clean and smooth.

609
00:35:26,780 --> 00:35:30,980
We've been using this red clay as an exfoliant for many, many years.

610
00:35:31,480 --> 00:35:35,080
That's the therapeutic benefit, but mostly it's just plain fun.

611
00:35:35,081 --> 00:35:45,320
In Calistoga, California, the Golden Haven Spa is one of many in the area,

612
00:35:45,820 --> 00:35:47,560
offering another way to get covered in mud.

613
00:35:48,880 --> 00:35:54,360
Immersion in tubs filled with a hot, very thick, and somewhat sulfurous smelling concoction.

614
00:35:54,900 --> 00:35:59,660
It's a mix of clay, peat moss, and mineral water from the hot springs on the property.

615
00:35:59,661 --> 00:36:05,960
The peat moss, what it does is it helps keep you out float while the clay helps pull toxins

616
00:36:05,961 --> 00:36:10,200
out from the body, while the mineral water that we have here on premises helps relax

617
00:36:10,201 --> 00:36:15,040
the muscles from the heat and everything.

618
00:36:15,041 --> 00:36:20,780
Mud tubs here have pipes that carry the 145 degree mineral water that help keep the mud

619
00:36:20,781 --> 00:36:25,040
tubs very warm.

620
00:36:25,041 --> 00:36:28,420
After the allotted time for the treatment, it takes a bit of effort to work your way

621
00:36:28,421 --> 00:36:30,920
back out of the thick and heavy mud.

622
00:36:30,921 --> 00:36:33,220
Then it's into the mineral water-enrich showers

623
00:36:33,221 --> 00:36:34,020
to rinse it off.

624
00:36:39,260 --> 00:36:42,440
While they're showering, what we then do is come in,

625
00:36:42,441 --> 00:36:47,400
we mix the tubs, bank them, flood them.

626
00:36:47,401 --> 00:36:49,640
And this is a cleaning process that takes place

627
00:36:49,641 --> 00:36:52,320
after every use.

628
00:36:52,321 --> 00:36:56,140
Now we're all done and ready to get the next people.

629
00:36:56,141 --> 00:36:58,420
We have a strange relationship with dirt.

630
00:36:58,421 --> 00:37:00,920
We're willing to pay to be covered in mud.

631
00:37:00,921 --> 00:37:03,300
But what about all the dirt we don't want?

632
00:37:03,301 --> 00:37:06,420
For thousands of years, people have been asking the same question.

633
00:37:06,421 --> 00:37:08,420
How do we get this stuff off our clothes?

634
00:37:11,420 --> 00:37:13,420
Although we live in a world of dirt,

635
00:37:13,421 --> 00:37:18,420
we're obsessed with keeping it off our cars, our carpets, and especially our clothes.

636
00:37:18,421 --> 00:37:22,420
The average household does about 300 loads of laundry a year,

637
00:37:22,421 --> 00:37:27,420
and that means about 1,100 wash loads are started every second of every day in the United States.

638
00:37:27,421 --> 00:37:29,866
United States. That's a lot of laundry.

639
00:37:29,867 --> 00:37:32,940
For over half a century, one detergent has washed

640
00:37:32,941 --> 00:37:34,460
more clothes than any other.

641
00:37:34,461 --> 00:37:39,700
Todd, launched in 1946, was the first synthetic laundry detergent that was launched on a

642
00:37:39,701 --> 00:37:40,700
national basis.

643
00:37:40,701 --> 00:37:44,660
Todd is dirt out there.

644
00:37:44,661 --> 00:37:51,860
It skyrocketed to market leadership because of its ability to take out and remove dirt.

645
00:37:51,861 --> 00:37:56,620
Ohio is home to one of Procter & Gamble's main research facilities.

646
00:37:56,621 --> 00:38:02,020
In this facility, we have roughly 600 appliances, washers and dryers, helping us do over 20,000

647
00:38:02,021 --> 00:38:03,700
loads of laundry in a year.

648
00:38:03,701 --> 00:38:07,980
Doing this many loads enables us to determine how we can best modify our formula to remove

649
00:38:07,981 --> 00:38:08,980
more dirt.

650
00:38:08,981 --> 00:38:14,360
At laboratory workstations in the building's analytical sciences department, chemists and

651
00:38:14,361 --> 00:38:19,440
other researchers study fabrics, the substances that soil and stain them, and chemical and

652
00:38:19,441 --> 00:38:21,800
enzyme cleaning agents.

653
00:38:21,801 --> 00:38:24,260
Then they test how they interact with each other.

654
00:38:24,261 --> 00:38:27,640
determined to root out even the smallest particles of dirt.

655
00:38:27,641 --> 00:38:30,320
We're in the microscopy lab at Procter & Gamble,

656
00:38:30,321 --> 00:38:33,820
and we have a cotton sock that today we've cut a piece out.

657
00:38:33,821 --> 00:38:37,160
We've mounted that piece inside the electron microscope,

658
00:38:37,161 --> 00:38:39,640
and we're going to start looking at it at about 30x,

659
00:38:39,641 --> 00:38:42,760
and we'll work our way up to high magnifications.

660
00:38:42,761 --> 00:38:45,920
At 30 times, we only see the sock fibers,

661
00:38:45,921 --> 00:38:48,320
and when we zoom in to 500 times, then

662
00:38:48,321 --> 00:38:51,540
we can see the dirt really buried inside the fibers

663
00:38:51,541 --> 00:38:53,480
actually around all the fibers.

664
00:38:55,180 --> 00:38:56,760
Now we're in a clump of dirt,

665
00:38:56,980 --> 00:38:58,560
and we're going to zoom in onto that.

666
00:38:58,900 --> 00:39:01,380
At 10,000 times, to a trained eye,

667
00:39:01,760 --> 00:39:04,100
the dirt's composition is clearly a type of clay.

668
00:39:05,320 --> 00:39:06,980
And then we do an X-ray,

669
00:39:07,080 --> 00:39:09,140
and we can see the components that make up that,

670
00:39:09,200 --> 00:39:10,440
or the elements that make it up,

671
00:39:10,500 --> 00:39:13,080
which are magnesium, calcium, and iron,

672
00:39:13,540 --> 00:39:15,560
and that's what the components are

673
00:39:15,561 --> 00:39:17,160
that's in the clay that we're seeing

674
00:39:17,161 --> 00:39:18,720
on the actual cotton sock.

675
00:39:18,721 --> 00:39:20,140
By doing this analysis,

676
00:39:20,141 --> 00:39:24,640
we can better target the detergents and the enzymes required to remove those.

677
00:39:24,940 --> 00:39:29,760
One of the major cleaning components that helps lift out embedded dirt is called a surfactant.

678
00:39:30,580 --> 00:39:32,860
A surfactant stands for surface active agent.

679
00:39:33,380 --> 00:39:38,700
A surface active agent, or a surfactant, is one molecule that has two very unique and distinct properties.

680
00:39:39,560 --> 00:39:42,740
One end of a surfactant molecule is attracted to water.

681
00:39:43,220 --> 00:39:49,580
The other end is hydrophobic and attaches itself to anything else, including dirt, oil, and grease.

682
00:39:49,581 --> 00:39:53,580
The agitation in washing machines helps create a tug of war.

683
00:39:53,581 --> 00:39:57,580
The opposing forces of the surfactant wet the dirt and work it loose from the fabric.

684
00:39:57,581 --> 00:40:04,580
Over the years, Tide has added new enzymes to attack specific stains and diversified with different fragrances.

685
00:40:04,581 --> 00:40:09,580
But after almost four decades, it introduced the biggest change.

686
00:40:09,581 --> 00:40:12,004
In 1984, Tide launched liquid detergent.

687
00:40:12,005 --> 00:40:15,580
At the time, the predominant form of the market was powder.

688
00:40:15,581 --> 00:40:18,580
But fast forward to today, Tide is the dominant player.

689
00:40:18,581 --> 00:40:25,120
The biggest manufacturing facility for liquid detergent in the world is Procter & Gamble's facility in Lima, Ohio.

690
00:40:27,580 --> 00:40:33,300
Trucks and train cars deliver the huge amounts of what's needed to make up the different laundry products manufactured here,

691
00:40:33,760 --> 00:40:38,200
including the complex formulations of Tide that contain over 50 ingredients.

692
00:40:40,340 --> 00:40:45,040
The Lima plant's packing lines run 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

693
00:40:45,041 --> 00:40:59,041
every minute one packing line outputs enough tide to last a family of four for 20 years

694
00:41:03,400 --> 00:41:08,800
because of the volume of product trucked instead of driving on public streets

695
00:41:08,801 --> 00:41:14,180
procter and gamble built their own mile and a half long road to their 1.1 million square foot

696
00:41:14,181 --> 00:41:16,080
fully automated warehouse.

697
00:41:16,081 --> 00:41:19,320
The Empire State Building would fit inside this building,

698
00:41:19,321 --> 00:41:22,220
still with room for football fields on either end.

699
00:41:22,221 --> 00:41:25,060
It never shuts down, it never takes a break,

700
00:41:25,061 --> 00:41:28,180
and it's always ready to do what we need it to do.

701
00:41:28,181 --> 00:41:30,020
Forklifts unload the pallets of Tide

702
00:41:30,021 --> 00:41:32,980
and other Procter & Gamble products off the trucks

703
00:41:32,981 --> 00:41:34,920
and into the automated shelving system.

704
00:41:39,720 --> 00:41:43,200
Each pallet is transported to one of the 94,000 storage

705
00:41:43,201 --> 00:41:46,360
positions inside this dark cavernous maze.

706
00:41:46,361 --> 00:41:47,760
No need for lights.

707
00:41:47,761 --> 00:41:49,600
Computers and robots are doing all the work.

708
00:41:52,340 --> 00:41:54,000
When an order needs to be filled,

709
00:41:54,001 --> 00:41:58,160
it's delivered to the other side of the facility.

710
00:41:58,161 --> 00:42:00,800
We're at the outbound end of the automated warehouse.

711
00:42:00,801 --> 00:42:04,040
An order has been automatically staged here by the computer.

712
00:42:04,041 --> 00:42:06,840
That signals a driver to come and begin unloading.

713
00:42:06,841 --> 00:42:09,240
In just a few minutes, this laundry product

714
00:42:09,241 --> 00:42:10,760
will be on its way.

715
00:42:10,761 --> 00:42:14,700
25% of the laundry loads completed in the United States

716
00:42:14,701 --> 00:42:17,300
are completed with products shipped out of this warehouse.

717
00:42:19,200 --> 00:42:21,440
One thing that laundry detergent manufacturers

718
00:42:21,441 --> 00:42:25,380
can count on is a continuing demand for their products.

719
00:42:25,381 --> 00:42:28,500
We always find plenty of ways to get dirty.

